// Files, as the machine's host sees them: list a directory, read a file,
// write one, delete one, and make or unmake a directory.
//
// **Why this exists at all.** `openspec/changes/program-run/design.md`
// rejected file frames — the client and `lcnc-flatserver` run on one host on
// the panel, so the browser can read the filesystem directly and
// `ProgramOpen` hands task a path it can open. That is still true of the
// panel and is still the deployment. It is *not* true of a client on any
// other host, where the browser lists the wrong filesystem and every
// `ProgramOpen` fails at the machine, and it is not true of a program that
// arrives on a stick plugged into the panel while the interface runs
// somewhere else. These frames make the browser correct from anywhere.
//
// The rejection named four hazards, and each is answered here rather than
// left to an implementation: **path escapes** by `roots` (below),
// **symlinks** by every path being answered resolved and by `DirEntry`
// carrying `resolved` beside `path`, **large files** by
// `ServerInfo.max_file_bytes` and by geometry still travelling as a bulk
// file, **partial reads** by `FileRead.offset` / `max_bytes` and
// `FileReadValue.truncated`.
//
// **The server serves paths; it does not decide what an operator may see.**
// The exclusion set, the hidden-name rule, and the confinement of writes to
// the program tree are the client's — `foggy-ui/packages/machine`'s
// `program/browse.ts`, where they are pure and tested — for the same reason
// every other guard is the client's. What the server enforces is the one
// thing a client cannot: that a path it is asked for is under a root the
// machine declared. Those are two different rules and neither is a copy of
// the other.
//
// **Roots.** The server answers only for paths under
// `ServerInfo.file_roots`, resolved, compared component by component. A
// path outside them is refused by name, before it is opened, and refused
// identically whether the connection came over the Unix socket or over
// `--tcp`, which widens nothing.
//
// **The default root is the machine ini's own directory**, and it needs no
// configuration: the server is already in that directory when it reads
// this, so the safe answer is the one a machine gets by saying nothing.
// A `[FILES]ROOT` line in `lcnc-flatserver.ini` — the same document the
// HAL pins are declared in — **replaces** that default rather than adding
// to it, so a machine that names its roots gets exactly those. Only a
// session with no machine ini to default to (`--nml`) and no declaration
// serves nothing, answering `has_files = false` and refusing all five.
//
// **An observing connection reads and does not write.** `Hello.observe`
// says the connection watches, so `DirChange`, `FileUpload` and
// `FileDelete` are refused by name from it, and `DirRead` and `FileRead`
// are answered unchanged. It is the rule `HalWrite` already follows.
//
// **Every reply carries `error`, and non-empty means it failed** — the
// shape `PreviewResult` uses. There is no separate result enum: a
// filesystem call either did the thing or has a sentence saying why not,
// and the distinction `Ack` draws between *received* and *done* does not
// arise, because these frames are answered when the work is finished.
// `path` on a reply is what the server resolved, so a client can see what
// it actually touched.

include "common.fbs";

namespace Lcfb;

/// One entry in a listing.
///
/// `resolved` is `path` with every symbolic link followed, and it is a
/// separate field rather than a replacement because the browser needs both:
/// it shows and navigates `path`, and it matches its exclusion set against
/// `resolved`, so a link into an excluded tree is excluded by the same rule
/// and there is no second rule for links. Where the entry cannot be
/// resolved — a broken link — `resolved` is empty and `is_link` is true.
///
/// `size` and `mtime_ns` are of the entry itself, after resolution, on the
/// same clock and in the same units as `PreviewResult`, so a client can tell
/// a stale preview from a fresh one without a second stat.
table DirEntry {
  name: string;
  path: string;
  resolved: string;
  is_dir: bool;
  is_link: bool;
  size: ulong;
  mtime_ns: ulong;
}

/// List a directory.
///
/// **An empty `path` asks for the roots**, which is how a client reaches
/// the top of what is served without knowing where that is. Where more
/// than one root is configured the reply is one entry per root, named by
/// its label; where there is exactly **one** it is that root's contents
/// instead, because a chooser holding one row costs a keypress on a
/// five-key panel and tells the operator nothing.
/// `DirReadValue.path` tells the two apart — see there.
///
/// It carries no filter — no glob, no hidden-name flag, no sort. What is
/// hidden from an operator is the client's rule and it changes with the
/// screen; a filter here would be a second copy of it, in the process least
/// able to test it. The server returns every name `readdir` gave it and the
/// client shows what it means to show.
table DirRead {
  id: uint;
  path: string;
}

/// The listing. Entries are in `readdir` order — sorting is display, and
/// display is the client's.
///
/// An entry that could not be stat'ed is **omitted and counted**, never
/// fatal: one racing removal or one broken mount must not take away a
/// directory that is otherwise fine, and a client that wants to say
/// *something is here I cannot show you* has the count to say it with.
/// The directory itself failing is an `error`, because there is then
/// nothing to show.
table DirReadValue {
  id: uint;
  /// Non-empty means the listing failed, and why; nothing else is meaningful.
  error: string;
  /// The directory as the server resolved it — and **empty** when these
  /// entries are the roots themselves, which is the whole of how a client
  /// tells a roots listing from a directory's contents. A single-root
  /// server answering an empty `DirRead.path` sets it to that root, so the
  /// two answers to the same question stay distinguishable.
  path: string;
  entries: [DirEntry];
  /// How many names were returned by the kernel and left out of `entries`.
  omitted: uint;
}

/// What a `DirChange` does. One message rather than three, because the
/// three share every field but one and one refusal path.
enum DirChangeKind : byte {
  /// `mkdir`. Fails when something is already at `path`.
  Create = 0,
  /// `rmdir`, and only that: an **empty** directory. Never recursive —
  /// a recursive delete over a socket is one typo away from the machine's
  /// configuration, and no screen has ever asked for one.
  Remove = 1,
  /// `rename`, which is atomic but only within one filesystem: a rename
  /// from a stick to the program tree is refused by name rather than
  /// silently copied, because the caller asked for a rename.
  Rename = 2
}

table DirChange {
  id: uint;
  kind: DirChangeKind;
  path: string;
  /// `Rename` only: the new path. Under a root like every other path.
  to: string;
  /// `Create` only: create missing parents (`mkdir -p`). Off by default,
  /// so a mistyped path is an error rather than a new tree.
  parents: bool = false;
}

table DirChangeAck {
  id: uint;
  error: string;
  /// The directory the change left behind, resolved — the one created, or
  /// the new name after a rename. Empty after a `Remove`.
  path: string;
}

/// Read a file.
///
/// `offset` and `max_bytes` exist so a client can look at the head of a
/// program it has no intention of loading, and so a file larger than the
/// server's cap can be read at all. Zero `max_bytes` means the cap,
/// `ServerInfo.max_file_bytes`; a larger value is clamped to it rather than
/// refused, and `truncated` then says so. An `offset` past the end is not
/// an error: it reads nothing.
table FileRead {
  id: uint;
  path: string;
  offset: ulong = 0;
  max_bytes: uint = 0;
}

/// The bytes, and the identity of what they were read from.
///
/// `content` is bytes rather than a string: a G-code file is text by
/// convention and not by guarantee, and a decoder in the client can fail
/// loudly where a decoder here would have to guess silently.
///
/// `size` and `mtime_ns` are the **whole file's**, sampled at the read, so
/// a partial read still tells the client what it is looking into and a
/// later write can notice the file moved underneath it.
table FileReadValue {
  id: uint;
  error: string;
  /// The file as the server resolved it.
  path: string;
  content: [ubyte];
  size: ulong;
  mtime_ns: ulong;
  /// There are bytes after `offset + content.length`.
  truncated: bool;
}

/// Write a file whole, replacing whatever was there.
///
/// **The write is atomic**: the server writes a temporary in the
/// destination's own directory and renames it, so an interrupted upload
/// leaves the previous file intact. The temporary is in that directory and
/// not in `/tmp` because `rename` is atomic only within a filesystem. This
/// is the rule the client already follows for its own files
/// (`program_files.ts`), stated here because it now happens in two places.
///
/// There is no chunking and no resume. A program is bounded — it is a file
/// a person wrote — and `ServerInfo.max_file_bytes` says by how much; a
/// larger upload is refused by name and stays refused. Bulk that is not
/// bounded, which is preview geometry, still never crosses the stream: it
/// is a file the client is told the name of.
table FileUpload {
  id: uint;
  path: string;
  content: [ubyte];
  /// Refuse when anything is already at `path`, rather than replacing it.
  /// The overwrite confirmation is the client's — this is what makes the
  /// answer it got still true at the write.
  create_only: bool = false;
  /// Create missing parent directories. Off by default, for `DirChange`'s
  /// reason.
  parents: bool = false;
}

table FileUploadAck {
  id: uint;
  error: string;
  /// The file as the server resolved it, after the rename.
  path: string;
  size: ulong;
  mtime_ns: ulong;
  /// A file was there and has been replaced. False with `create_only`.
  replaced: bool;
}

/// Delete one file. Not a directory — that is `DirChange.Remove` — and
/// never a tree.
table FileDelete {
  id: uint;
  path: string;
  /// Succeed when there was nothing there. A client removing a scratch
  /// file it may never have written sets it; one deleting what the
  /// operator pointed at does not, and hears that it was already gone.
  missing_ok: bool = false;
}

table FileDeleteAck {
  id: uint;
  error: string;
  /// The file as the server resolved it, before the unlink.
  path: string;
  /// It was there and is gone. False when `missing_ok` covered an absence.
  existed: bool;
}
